// src/kernel/core/cap/kind_audio_controller.pdx — R33.M1-005 (#1141)
//
// KIND_AUDIO_CONTROLLER = 0x15e, derived over KIND_DEVICE (10).
//
// The authority to hold one HDA controller's static identity
// (hda_key, bar_handle, corb_size, rirb_size) and to be told when the
// codec-discovery scan updated the codec count on that controller.
//
// =============================================================================
// §0  UNITS: CODEC_COUNT IS A DIMENSIONLESS ORDINAL IN [0, 15]
// =============================================================================
//
// Intel HDA §4.3 caps the number of codecs a single controller may host at
// FIFTEEN (one codec per SDIN line, and SDIN0..SDIN14 are the fifteen
// wires the controller electrically supports; SDIN15 does not exist on
// any Intel southbridge). A codec_count that could be raised past 15
// would let the codec-discovery scan report a codec on a line the
// controller cannot address, and every future GET_PARAMETER against it
// would silently address codec address 0 with a wraparound the hardware
// does not perform.
//
// THE COUNT IS STORED AS-IS AND NEVER INTERPRETED. There is no
// conversion in the module -- comparisons, storage and the report
// installer are all in the same ordinal, so the per-controller meaning
// is preserved verbatim.
//
// =============================================================================
// §1  STATIC IDENTITY IS AT MINT, DYNAMIC STATE IS INSTALLED
// =============================================================================
//
// Same discipline as `kind_backlight.pdx` §1 and `kind_battery.pdx` §1.
// `bar_handle`, `corb_size` and `rirb_size` are ROW IDENTITY -- they
// name the controller's register window and its enumerated ring shapes
// -- and are refused to a mint that supplies implausible values. The
// dynamic field (codec_count, report count) starts at zero and is
// updated by `audio_ctrl_report_install`, REACHABLE ONLY THROUGH THE
// ROUTED ASSESSMENT: capability in, one ordinal in, no other parameter.
//
// WHAT `brightness_max` IS TO A BACKLIGHT, `corb_size` IS TO AN AUDIO
// CONTROLLER. It is set once by the mint and there is no primitive
// anywhere that changes it. A corb_size that could be raised by a
// caller would let the discovery driver walk a ring the controller
// never programmed; a corb_size that could be lowered would let a live
// producer clobber verbs the controller was still expected to consume.
//
// =============================================================================
// §2  DERIVATION OVER KIND_DEVICE — THE EIGHTH TIME THE KIND HALF PROVES
//     NOTHING
// =============================================================================
//
// The gate demands `kind == KIND_DEVICE (10)` carrying `RIGHT_MINT`. As
// with KIND_THERMAL_ZONE, KIND_BATTERY, KIND_COOLING_DEVICE,
// KIND_BACKLIGHT and KIND_HID_DEVICE, that half discriminates NOTHING
// on its own: every PCI function in the machine is a KIND_DEVICE, and
// "this device is an HDA controller" is a firmware-namespace-plus-
// class-code fact this ring may not distill in isolation (the PCI
// probe reports class 04:03; the supervisor that walked the namespace
// is what MINTED the device capability). The kernel enforces exactly
// what it can:
//
//   * The inherited device key must be NON-ZERO
//     (`AUDIO_CTRL_MINT_NO_DEVICE`).
//   * NO OTHER LIVE ROW MAY HOLD THAT KEY
//     (`AUDIO_CTRL_MINT_AC_IN_USE`). One controller, one capability --
//     two capabilities over one controller means two drivers writing
//     one BAR window, and the one that writes last decides.
//   * corb_size and rirb_size are enumerated 0..2 -- the three legal
//     HDA_CORB_SIZE_* / HDA_RIRB_SIZE_* codes.
//
// The discrimination this gate does not perform is performed by the
// ring-3 supervisor (or the pci_enumerator that identified class 04:03)
// that walked the namespace, and the capability is the record of ITS
// decision.
//
// =============================================================================
// §3  A GARBAGE CODEC-COUNT REPORT IS DANGEROUS
// =============================================================================
//
// A codec_count report comes through the codec-discovery driver.
//
//   * A count above 15 that is silently CLAMPED hides a broken discovery
//     scan from the operator and pins the controller at 15 codecs
//     regardless of what the scan meant to say -- every GET_PARAMETER
//     for the imaginary codec addresses returns DEFERRED_HARDWARE
//     against a wire the controller does not have.
//
// So `audio_ctrl_count_valid` refuses anything > 15 with
// `AUDIO_CTRL_IMPLAUSIBLE_COUNT`, and the report installer counts the
// refusal. NOTHING IS CLAMPED anywhere in this path.
//
// =============================================================================
// §4  WHAT THIS KIND DOES NOT DO
// =============================================================================
//
//   * NOTHING DRIVES A CONTROLLER. `audio_ctrl_report_install` returns
//     AUDIO_CTRL_OK for any legal count; there is no MMIO or port I/O
//     path in this kind at all. Which BAR handle addresses which
//     register window, and how the discovery scan classifies presence,
//     lives in src/kernel/core/drivers/hda/*.pdx.
//   * NOTHING BINDS THE HARDWARE. The bar_handle field is an opaque
//     u64 recorded at mint; a live driver reaches the hardware via
//     hda_controller_bind, which this kind neither calls nor mediates.
//
// =============================================================================
// §5  NO PORT I/O, NO AML, NO FIRMWARE NAMES
// =============================================================================
//
// This module contains no `in`/`out` and no firmware-bytecode
// identifier. `tools/lint-no-kernel-aml.sh` refuses any kernel
// identifier matching `\baml`, `\bdsdt`, `\bssdt`, `\bacpica`, `\_SB_`
// or `\_SB.`. KIND_AUDIO_CONTROLLER, audio_ctrl_* and
// _audio_controller_table match none of the six.
//
// =============================================================================

module KindAudioController = structure {

  // ==========================================================================
  // The tag, and the parent kind.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let KIND_AUDIO_CONTROLLER : u64 = 0x15e  // derived-kind tag (350)
  pub let AUDIO_CTRL_PARENT_KIND : u64 = 10    // = KIND_DEVICE

  // ==========================================================================
  // §0 / §1 bounds.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let AUDIO_CTRL_CODEC_MAX  : u64 = 15     // Intel HDA §4.3: 15 SDIN lines
  pub let AUDIO_CTRL_RING_MAX   : u64 = 2      // 0=2, 1=16, 2=256 (HDA_CORB/RIRB_SIZE_*)

  // ==========================================================================
  // Rights.
  //
  // R_AUDIO_CTRL_READ / _REPORT / _INVOKE / _REVOKE / _OBSERVE.
  // RIGHT_MINT (0x200) is OUTSIDE the mask: this kind is a leaf.
  // Nothing in the tree derives from an audio-controller capability.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let R_AUDIO_CTRL_READ    : u64 = 0x001
  pub let R_AUDIO_CTRL_REPORT  : u64 = 0x002
  pub let R_AUDIO_CTRL_INVOKE  : u64 = 0x008
  pub let R_AUDIO_CTRL_REVOKE  : u64 = 0x010
  pub let R_AUDIO_CTRL_OBSERVE : u64 = 0x400
  pub let R_AUDIO_CTRL_ALL     : u64 = 0x41B

  // ==========================================================================
  // Ops (op_arg[7:0]) and the right each requires.
  //
  //   0 QUERY_KEY      INVOKE   the row's own hda_key
  //   1 QUERY_BAR      INVOKE   bar_handle
  //   2 QUERY_CODECS   INVOKE   codec_count
  //   3 QUERY_CORBSZ   INVOKE   corb_size ordinal
  //   4 QUERY_RIRBSZ   INVOKE   rirb_size ordinal
  //   5 QUERY_REPORTS  INVOKE   lifetime report count
  //   6 QUERY_PARENT   INVOKE   the parent KIND_DEVICE slot
  //   7 DEBUG_PRINT    OBSERVE  canonical row dump
  //   8 REPORT         REPORT   push {new_codec_count}
  //
  // REPORT packs one value in op_arg:
  //   op_arg[7:0]   = op = 8
  //   op_arg[11:8]  = new_codec_count (4 bits, 0..15)
  //   op_arg[63:12] = reserved, MUST be zero
  //
  // The reserved field is REFUSED with AUDIO_CTRL_TAIL_BAD_ARG rather
  // than masked, for the reason kind_backlight's REPORT reserved is:
  // fifty-two free bits on an op that reaches the row's cached count
  // are where a second value would be smuggled.
  //
  // Ops 0..7 take NO OPERAND. op_arg[63:8] is REFUSED rather than
  // masked, per the discipline in kind_hid_event.pdx §ops.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let AUDIO_CTRL_OP_QUERY_KEY     : u64 = 0
  pub let AUDIO_CTRL_OP_QUERY_BAR     : u64 = 1
  pub let AUDIO_CTRL_OP_QUERY_CODECS  : u64 = 2
  pub let AUDIO_CTRL_OP_QUERY_CORBSZ  : u64 = 3
  pub let AUDIO_CTRL_OP_QUERY_RIRBSZ  : u64 = 4
  pub let AUDIO_CTRL_OP_QUERY_REPORTS : u64 = 5
  pub let AUDIO_CTRL_OP_QUERY_PARENT  : u64 = 6
  pub let AUDIO_CTRL_OP_DEBUG_PRINT   : u64 = 7
  pub let AUDIO_CTRL_OP_REPORT        : u64 = 8
  pub let AUDIO_CTRL_OP_MAX           : u64 = 8

  // ==========================================================================
  // Failure taxonomy — 0xFFFFFEB0..0xFFFFFEBF.
  //
  // Sixteen-wide, disjoint from KIND_SENSOR_CHANNEL (0xFFFFFEA0..AF),
  // KIND_HID_EVENT (0xFFFFFE90..9F), KIND_HID_DEVICE (0xFFFFFE80..8F),
  // KIND_BACKLIGHT (0xFFFFFE70..7F), battery_monitor (0xFFFFFE60..6F),
  // KIND_COOLING_DEVICE (0xFFFFFE50..5F), KIND_BATTERY
  // (0xFFFFFE40..4F), KIND_THERMAL_ZONE (0xFFFFFE30..3F), owner
  // (0xFFFFFE2B..2F), thermal policy (0xFFFFFE20..2A), KIND_FW_SESSION
  // (0xFFFFFE10..1F), KIND_EC_QUERY (0xFFFFFE00..0F). Disjointness is
  // what lets a u64 threaded up through the mint, the row allocator and
  // the dispatch handler still name the gate that refused it.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let AUDIO_CTRL_OK                 : u64 = 0
  pub let AUDIO_CTRL_TAIL_ENOSPC        : u64 = 0xFFFFFEBF
  pub let AUDIO_CTRL_TAIL_BAD_ARG       : u64 = 0xFFFFFEBE
  pub let AUDIO_CTRL_MINT_BAD_PARENT    : u64 = 0xFFFFFEBD
  pub let AUDIO_CTRL_MINT_BAD_RIGHTS    : u64 = 0xFFFFFEBC
  pub let AUDIO_CTRL_MINT_BAD_CORB      : u64 = 0xFFFFFEBB
  pub let AUDIO_CTRL_MINT_BAD_RIRB      : u64 = 0xFFFFFEBA
  pub let AUDIO_CTRL_MINT_NO_DEVICE     : u64 = 0xFFFFFEB8
  pub let AUDIO_CTRL_MINT_ENOSPC        : u64 = 0xFFFFFEB7
  pub let AUDIO_CTRL_MINT_AC_IN_USE     : u64 = 0xFFFFFEB6
  pub let AUDIO_CTRL_BAD_SLOT           : u64 = 0xFFFFFEB5
  pub let AUDIO_CTRL_WRONG_KIND         : u64 = 0xFFFFFEB4
  pub let AUDIO_CTRL_REVOKE_ALREADY     : u64 = 0xFFFFFEB3
  pub let AUDIO_CTRL_IMPLAUSIBLE_COUNT  : u64 = 0xFFFFFEB2

  // The decoders' contract for a dead or out-of-range row.
  pub let AUDIO_CTRL_DECODE_BAD : u64 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF

  // `audio_ctrl_find_by_key`'s "no row holds this key". 0xFF rather than
  // 0, because row 0 is a legitimate answer.
  pub let AUDIO_CTRL_ROW_NONE : u64 = 0xFF

  // ==========================================================================
  // Table sizing and row layout.
  //
  // TWO ROWS, sized by HDA CONTROLLERS A MACHINE HAS. A T14 G4 declares
  // one (the PCH cAVS controller); two allows a discrete audio card
  // alongside.
  //
  // Sixty-four bytes per row so the index is a `shl 6`; paideia-as has
  // no `mul`.
  //
  //   [+0]  header word
  //           bits [7:0]   reserved, must be zero
  //           bits [15:8]  parent_slot  cap_table slot of the parent
  //                                     KIND_DEVICE at mint; audit and
  //                                     query only
  //           bits [55:16] reserved, must be zero
  //           bits [63:56] in_use       allocator flag
  //   [+8]  hda_key          INHERITED from the parent descriptor's
  //                          target_ptr. Non-zero. The uniqueness key.
  //   [+16] bar_handle       opaque u64. May be zero at mint (matches
  //                          hda_controller_bind's opaque discipline).
  //   [+24] codec_count      0..AUDIO_CTRL_CODEC_MAX. DYNAMIC — the one
  //                          dynamic field on this row, updated by
  //                          audio_ctrl_report_install.
  //   [+32] corb_size        0..AUDIO_CTRL_RING_MAX (HDA_CORB_SIZE_*)
  //   [+40] rirb_size        0..AUDIO_CTRL_RING_MAX (HDA_RIRB_SIZE_*)
  //   [+48] reports          u64 lifetime install count
  //   [+56] reserved, zero
  //
  // `tools/build.sh` asserts by `objdump -r` that no object other than
  // `core/cap/kind_audio_controller.o` relocates against
  // `_audio_controller_table`. The table is the only place in the
  // kernel where an HDA controller's bar_handle and ring sizes live,
  // and a second writer could restamp them with no capability involved
  // and no refusal recorded.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let AUDIO_CTRL_MAX       : u64 = 2
  pub let AUDIO_CTRL_ROW_BYTES : u64 = 64

  pub let mut _audio_controller_table : [u64; 16] = uninit @align(64)

  // Counters. Deliberately NOT in the row: they survive a revoke, so
  // "this controller keeps returning nonsense" is evidence about a
  // controller and outlives any one capability over it.
  //
  //   +0  mints          successful mints
  //   +8  reports        install successes
  //   +16 impl_count     reports refused for count > CODEC_MAX
  //   +24 in_use         mints refused because key already held
  //   +32 enospc         mints refused because table full
  //   +40 revokes        successful revokes
  //   +48 reserved
  //   +56 reserved
  pub let mut _audio_ctrl_stats : [u64; 8] = uninit @align(64)

  pub let AUDIO_CTRL_ST_MINTS      : u64 = 0
  pub let AUDIO_CTRL_ST_REPORTS    : u64 = 1
  pub let AUDIO_CTRL_ST_IMPL_COUNT : u64 = 2
  pub let AUDIO_CTRL_ST_IN_USE     : u64 = 3
  pub let AUDIO_CTRL_ST_ENOSPC     : u64 = 4
  pub let AUDIO_CTRL_ST_REVOKES    : u64 = 5
  pub let AUDIO_CTRL_ST_MAX        : u64 = 8

  // Local klog keys and tags.
  pub let ac_dbg_msg : [u8; 16] = "AUDIO CTRL ROW\n\0"
  pub let ac_k_row   : [u8; 4]  = "row\0"
  pub let ac_k_key   : [u8; 4]  = "key\0"
  pub let ac_k_bar   : [u8; 4]  = "bar\0"
  pub let ac_k_cdc   : [u8; 5]  = "cdcs\0"
  pub let ac_k_cbs   : [u8; 5]  = "cbsz\0"
  pub let ac_k_rbs   : [u8; 5]  = "rbsz\0"

  // ==========================================================================
  // audio_ctrl_table_reset() -> ()
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let audio_ctrl_table_reset : () -> () !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R33.M1-005 (#1141): clear every row and every counter. Boot and teardown only. Two separate store runs rather than one over the sum, mirroring backlight/battery/thermal table_reset: the two arrays have different lengths and one loop over their total would encode their adjacency in .bss as a fact the code depends on. Bounded by immediate compares, 16 iterations for the row table and 8 for the counters. Leaf. Labels prefixed ac_rs_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        lea r10, [rip + _audio_controller_table];
        xor rax, rax;
        xor rcx, rcx;
      ac_rs_rows:
        mov [r10 + rcx * 8], rax;
        add rcx, 1;
        cmp rcx, 16;
        jb  ac_rs_rows;

        lea r10, [rip + _audio_ctrl_stats];
        xor rcx, rcx;
      ac_rs_stats:
        mov [r10 + rcx * 8], rax;
        add rcx, 1;
        cmp rcx, 8;
        jb  ac_rs_stats;
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  // ==========================================================================
  // audio_ctrl_corb_size_valid(sz) -> 1 | 0
  // audio_ctrl_rirb_size_valid(sz) -> 1 | 0
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let audio_ctrl_corb_size_valid : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (sz: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R33.M1-005 (#1141): 1 iff sz in [0, AUDIO_CTRL_RING_MAX]. §2's enumerated ring code -- the three legal HDA_CORB_SIZE_* codes. A size code outside 0..2 would not just render as a nonsense number in a debug dump; the CORB register bits [1:0] would still consume a value 3 as the reserved bit pattern and the controller's DMA engine would fail silently. Refused rather than defaulted. Pure leaf. Labels prefixed ac_cv_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        mov r10, 2;                      // AUDIO_CTRL_RING_MAX
        cmp rdi, r10;
        ja  ac_cv_no;
        mov rax, 1;
        ret;
      ac_cv_no:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  pub let audio_ctrl_rirb_size_valid : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (sz: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R33.M1-005 (#1141): 1 iff sz in [0, AUDIO_CTRL_RING_MAX]. Same discipline as audio_ctrl_corb_size_valid; declared separately so a future change to one ring's legal set does not silently widen the other. Pure leaf. Labels prefixed ac_rv_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        mov r10, 2;                      // AUDIO_CTRL_RING_MAX
        cmp rdi, r10;
        ja  ac_rv_no;
        mov rax, 1;
        ret;
      ac_rv_no:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  // ==========================================================================
  // audio_ctrl_count_valid(count) -> 1 | 0
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let audio_ctrl_count_valid : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (count: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R33.M1-005 (#1141): 1 iff count in [0, AUDIO_CTRL_CODEC_MAX]. §3's plausibility check. A count above 15 would let the discovery driver report a codec on a line the controller cannot address. Zero is admitted (a controller with no attached codec is a legitimate discovery outcome and different from 'no discovery ran'). Pure leaf. Labels prefixed ac_nv_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        mov r10, 15;                     // AUDIO_CTRL_CODEC_MAX
        cmp rdi, r10;
        ja  ac_nv_no;
        mov rax, 1;
        ret;
      ac_nv_no:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  // ==========================================================================
  // audio_ctrl_note(which) -> ()
  // audio_ctrl_stat(which) -> count
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let audio_ctrl_note : (u64) -> () !{mem} @{} =
    fn (which: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R33.M1-005 (#1141): increment one counter; silent no-op on out-of-range. Same shape as backlight_note / hid_device_note: every caller inside this module passes a constant, and the bound is here so the array cannot be walked past if that ever stops being true. Leaf. Labels prefixed ac_nt_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 8;                      // AUDIO_CTRL_ST_MAX
        jae ac_nt_out;
        lea r10, [rip + _audio_ctrl_stats];
        mov rax, [r10 + rdi * 8];
        add rax, 1;
        mov [r10 + rdi * 8], rax;
      ac_nt_out:
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  pub let audio_ctrl_stat : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (which: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R33.M1-005 (#1141): bounds-checked read of one counter; out of range returns 0. Leaf. Labels prefixed ac_st_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 8;                      // AUDIO_CTRL_ST_MAX
        jae ac_st_zero;
        lea r10, [rip + _audio_ctrl_stats];
        mov rax, [r10 + rdi * 8];
        ret;
      ac_st_zero:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  // ==========================================================================
  // audio_ctrl_rights_valid(rights) -> 1 | 0
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let audio_ctrl_rights_valid : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (rights: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R33.M1-005 (#1141): 1 iff (rights & R_AUDIO_CTRL_ALL) == rights. R_AUDIO_CTRL_ALL = 0x41B (READ|REPORT|INVOKE|REVOKE|OBSERVE). A subset test, and RIGHT_MINT (0x200) is OUTSIDE the mask: this kind is a leaf of the lattice, and a MINT bit no gate reads is authority that later acquires a meaning nobody audited. Pure leaf. Labels prefixed ac_rvv_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        mov rax, rdi;
        mov r10, 0x41B;                  // R_AUDIO_CTRL_ALL
        and rax, r10;
        cmp rax, rdi;
        jne ac_rvv_no;
        mov rax, 1;
        ret;
      ac_rvv_no:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  // ==========================================================================
  // audio_ctrl_check_parent_device(parent_slot) -> 1 | 0
  //
  // §2. The first half of the derivation gate. Discriminates NOTHING on
  // its own; the second half is in audio_ctrl_cap_mint_inner.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let audio_ctrl_check_parent_device : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{cap} =
    fn (parent_slot: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {cap},
      justification: "R33.M1-005 (#1141): the first half of the KIND_AUDIO_CONTROLLER derivation gate. Bounds-check parent_slot < 256; load the descriptor at cap_table + parent_slot*24 (stride 24 per cap/mint.pdx); require kind == KIND_DEVICE (10) AND (rights & RIGHT_MINT 0x200) != 0. THIS HALF DISCRIMINATES NOTHING and the mint does not treat it as though it did -- every PCI function in the machine is a KIND_DEVICE, which is #1075's lesson stated for the eighth time in this file family. 'This device is an HDA controller' is a class-code fact the ring-3 supervisor / pci_enumerator distills, and the capability is the record of ITS decision. Pure read. Labels prefixed ac_cpd_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        mov r11, 256;
        cmp rdi, r11;
        jae ac_cpd_no;

        mov rax, cap_table;
        mov r8, rdi;
        shl r8, 3;
        mov r9, rdi;
        shl r9, 4;
        add r8, r9;
        add rax, r8;

        mov r10, [rax + 0];
        cmp r10, 10;                     // KIND_DEVICE
        jne ac_cpd_no;

        mov r10, [rax + 8];
        mov r11, 0x200;                  // RIGHT_MINT
        and r10, r11;
        cmp r10, 0;
        je  ac_cpd_no;

        mov rax, 1;
        ret;

      ac_cpd_no:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // audio_ctrl_parent_key(parent_slot) -> hda_key | 0
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let audio_ctrl_parent_key : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{cap} =
    fn (parent_slot: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {cap},
      justification: "R33.M1-005 (#1141): read the parent KIND_DEVICE descriptor's target_ptr, which for a device capability is its identity key. THE ONLY SOURCE OF AN AUDIO CONTROLLER CAPABILITY'S IDENTITY: the mint has no device argument, so the controller whose static identity a row holds is the part its parent capability names and no other. A mint that took the key as an argument would let a holder attach one controller's ring shapes to a different controller -- and every future codec-discovery report thereafter would be confidently wrong about which BAR the driver's DMA was reaching. Returns 0 for a bad slot or a non-KIND_DEVICE descriptor, and audio_ctrl_cap_mint_inner refuses 0 with AUDIO_CTRL_MINT_NO_DEVICE. Pure read. Labels prefixed ac_pk_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        mov r11, 256;
        cmp rdi, r11;
        jae ac_pk_no;

        mov rax, cap_table;
        mov r8, rdi;
        shl r8, 3;
        mov r9, rdi;
        shl r9, 4;
        add r8, r9;
        add rax, r8;

        mov r10, [rax + 0];
        cmp r10, 10;                     // KIND_DEVICE
        jne ac_pk_no;

        mov rax, [rax + 16];             // target_ptr = device identity key
        ret;

      ac_pk_no:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // audio_ctrl_hdr_pack(parent_slot) -> hdr | AUDIO_CTRL_DECODE_BAD
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let audio_ctrl_hdr_pack : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (parent_slot: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R33.M1-005 (#1141): canonical header-word encoder. Validates parent_slot < 256 then packs (parent_slot << 8), leaving bits [7:0], [55:16] and [63:56] clear. Bits [63:56] are the allocator's in_use flag and MUST leave this function zero: a header arriving with in_use pre-set hands back a row the free scan skips forever, and on this kind the leaked row holds a bar_handle, so the next legitimate mint of that controller is refused AC_IN_USE by a ghost that is still the only record of the controller's ring shapes. Pure leaf. Labels prefixed ac_hp_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        mov r10, 256;
        cmp rdi, r10;
        jae ac_hp_bad;

        mov rax, rdi;
        shl rax, 8;
        ret;

      ac_hp_bad:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  pub let audio_ctrl_hdr_parent : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (hdr: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R33.M1-005 (#1141): parent_slot from header bits [15:8]. Named accessor rather than a shift at each call site. Pure leaf.",
      block: { mov rax, rdi; shr rax, 8; and rax, 0xFF; ret }
    } @no_frame

  // ==========================================================================
  // audio_ctrl_tail_alloc(hdr, hda_key, bar_handle, corb_size, rirb_size)
  //   -> row_id | AUDIO_CTRL_TAIL_*
  //
  // THE ONLY FUNCTION IN THE KERNEL THAT WRITES AN HDA CONTROLLER'S
  // BAR_HANDLE OR RING SHAPES.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let audio_ctrl_tail_alloc : (u64, u64, u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (hdr: u64) (hda_key: u64) (bar_handle: u64) (corb_size: u64) (rirb_size: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R33.M1-005 (#1141): claim one row. THE ONLY FUNCTION IN THE KERNEL THAT WRITES A BAR_HANDLE / CORB_SIZE / RIRB_SIZE, which is why tools/build.sh confines relocations against _audio_controller_table to this object. Gates, in order: hdr bits [63:56] non-zero -> TAIL_BAD_ARG (the allocator's in_use flag); hdr reserved bits [7:0] or [55:16] non-zero -> TAIL_BAD_ARG; hda_key == 0 -> TAIL_BAD_ARG (0 is audio_ctrl_parent_key's refusal value); corb_size > AUDIO_CTRL_RING_MAX -> TAIL_BAD_ARG; rirb_size > AUDIO_CTRL_RING_MAX -> TAIL_BAD_ARG. bar_handle is NOT validated -- it is an opaque handle mirroring hda_controller_bind's discipline, and a zero handle is legitimate at mint (the discovery pass will bind later). RANGE CHECKS ARE HERE AS WELL AS IN THE MINT, deliberately: the mint's versions produce specific codes an installer needs (MINT_BAD_CORB / MINT_BAD_RIRB), while this makes it impossible for ANY future second caller of the allocator to create a row without them. Low-first scan over 2 rows of 64 bytes, free iff byte 7 of [row+0] is zero. Claim writes all eight words, so a reused row cannot inherit the previous controller's cached codec count or report count. Five pushes leave rsp%16 == 0 at every nested call site (there are none in this leaf). Labels prefixed ac_ta_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;

        mov r12, rdi;                    // hdr
        mov r13, rsi;                    // hda_key
        mov r14, rdx;                    // bar_handle
        mov r15, rcx;                    // corb_size
        // rirb_size stays in r8

        // hdr in_use pre-set?
        mov rax, r12;
        shr rax, 56;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne ac_ta_bad;

        // hdr reserved [7:0]?
        mov rax, r12;
        and rax, 0xFF;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne ac_ta_bad;

        // hdr reserved [55:16]?
        mov rax, r12;
        shr rax, 16;
        mov r10, 0xFFFFFFFFFF;           // 40 bits [55:16]
        and rax, r10;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne ac_ta_bad;

        cmp r13, 0;
        je  ac_ta_bad;

        mov r10, 2;                      // AUDIO_CTRL_RING_MAX
        cmp r15, r10;
        ja  ac_ta_bad;
        cmp r8, r10;
        ja  ac_ta_bad;

        // Low-first free scan.
        xor rbx, rbx;                    // row_id
      ac_ta_scan:
        cmp rbx, 2;                      // AUDIO_CTRL_MAX
        jae ac_ta_enospc;

        lea r10, [rip + _audio_controller_table];
        mov r11, rbx;
        shl r11, 6;
        add r10, r11;

        mov rax, [r10 + 0];
        shr rax, 56;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  ac_ta_claim;

        add rbx, 1;
        jmp ac_ta_scan;

      ac_ta_claim:
        mov rax, r12;
        mov r11, 1;
        shl r11, 56;
        or  rax, r11;                    // in_use = 1
        mov [r10 + 0],  rax;
        mov [r10 + 8],  r13;             // hda_key
        mov [r10 + 16], r14;             // bar_handle
        xor rax, rax;
        mov [r10 + 24], rax;             // codec_count = 0
        mov [r10 + 32], r15;             // corb_size
        mov [r10 + 40], r8;              // rirb_size
        xor rax, rax;
        mov [r10 + 48], rax;             // reports = 0
        mov [r10 + 56], rax;             // reserved
        mov rax, rbx;
        jmp ac_ta_out;

      ac_ta_enospc:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFEBF;             // AUDIO_CTRL_TAIL_ENOSPC
        jmp ac_ta_out;
      ac_ta_bad:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFEBE;             // AUDIO_CTRL_TAIL_BAD_ARG

      ac_ta_out:
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let audio_ctrl_tail_valid : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (row_id: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R33.M1-005 (#1141): 1 iff row_id < AUDIO_CTRL_MAX and the row's in_use byte is set. Every decoder gates on this so that a freed row answers AUDIO_CTRL_DECODE_BAD rather than the zeroes it was scrubbed to. Leaf. Labels prefixed ac_tv_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 2;                      // AUDIO_CTRL_MAX
        jae ac_tv_no;
        lea r10, [rip + _audio_controller_table];
        mov r11, rdi;
        shl r11, 6;
        add r10, r11;
        mov rax, [r10 + 0];
        shr rax, 56;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  ac_tv_no;
        mov rax, 1;
        ret;
      ac_tv_no:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  pub let audio_ctrl_tail_free : (u64) -> () !{mem} @{} =
    fn (row_id: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R33.M1-005 (#1141): scrub one row to zero, which clears in_use and returns it to the free scan. ALL EIGHT WORDS, including the cached codec count and lifetime report count: a revoke is the point at which somebody has decided this controller's history no longer applies, and a row that kept its cached count across a re-mint would report a controller as hosting codecs on the strength of a report about a capability that no longer exists. Scrubbing the KEY is what makes the controller re-mintable. No-op on an out-of-range row. Leaf. Labels prefixed ac_tf_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 2;                      // AUDIO_CTRL_MAX
        jae ac_tf_out;
        lea r10, [rip + _audio_controller_table];
        mov r11, rdi;
        shl r11, 6;
        add r10, r11;
        xor rax, rax;
        mov [r10 + 0],  rax;
        mov [r10 + 8],  rax;
        mov [r10 + 16], rax;
        mov [r10 + 24], rax;
        mov [r10 + 32], rax;
        mov [r10 + 40], rax;
        mov [r10 + 48], rax;
        mov [r10 + 56], rax;
      ac_tf_out:
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  // ==========================================================================
  // Row accessors. Every one returns AUDIO_CTRL_DECODE_BAD for a dead row.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let audio_ctrl_row_hdr : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (row_id: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R33.M1-005 (#1141): raw header word of a live row, or AUDIO_CTRL_DECODE_BAD. Labels prefixed ac_rh_.",
      block: {
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        mov r12, rdi;
        call audio_ctrl_tail_valid;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  ac_rh_bad;
        lea r10, [rip + _audio_controller_table];
        mov r11, r12;
        shl r11, 6;
        add r10, r11;
        mov rax, [r10 + 0];
        jmp ac_rh_out;
      ac_rh_bad:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;
      ac_rh_out:
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret
      }
    }

